---
title: Policy
id: policy
order: 7
description: "Set allow/ask/deny guardrails over the commands and capabilities the in-sandbox agent may run, ask about, or never run, in one portable description that each harness maps onto its native permissions."
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A policy is your guardrail layer: it decides which commands and capabilities the\nin-sandbox agent may run outright, must ask about first, or can never run.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineSandboxPolicy()"},{"type":"text","value":" describes those rules once, portably, and each\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"provider"}]},{"type":"text","value":"'s harness adapter maps them onto its own native\npermission system. You attach a policy to a sandbox via\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineSandbox({ policy })"}]},{"type":"text","value":", where it guards the commands the\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":" setup and bridged "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" run."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandboxPolicy, defineSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n  capabilities: { fileWrite: 'allow', network: 'ask' },\n  default: 'ask',\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  policy,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"decisions","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decisions"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Every command or capability resolves to one of three decisions:"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Decision"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Meaning"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The agent runs it without interruption."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The agent pauses; the harness emits an approval request the client answers before the action proceeds."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The action is blocked outright. The agent cannot run it."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"commands","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Commands"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commands"},{"type":"text","value":" holds three lists of command patterns: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":".\nA pattern matches against the command the agent is about to run:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"glob-patterns","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Glob patterns"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Patterns support "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"*"},{"type":"text","value":" globs, so you can gate a whole family of commands with one\nentry. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"curl *"},{"type":"text","value":" matches any "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"curl"},{"type":"text","value":" invocation; "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo *"},{"type":"text","value":" matches anything run\nthrough "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo"},{"type":"text","value":". An exact string like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm test"},{"type":"text","value":" matches only that command.\nPrefer the named "},{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace scripts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm test"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm build"},{"type":"text","value":")\nin your "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":" list. They give the policy stable names to match rather than\nfreeform shell."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"capabilities","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Capabilities"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"capabilities"},{"type":"text","value":" applies the same "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":" decisions to\ncoarse-grained abilities rather than individual commands, for example\nfilesystem writes or outbound network access:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  capabilities: {\n    fileWrite: 'allow', // let the agent edit the working tree freely\n    network: 'ask',     // pause for approval before any outbound request\n  },\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is the broad backstop: even if a specific network command isn't in your\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commands"},{"type":"text","value":" lists, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"network: 'ask'"},{"type":"text","value":" still forces an approval for anything that\nreaches out."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some providers also enforce network at create time when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"network: 'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":". See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for that mapping."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"network-policy-on-docker-sandboxes","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Network policy on Docker Sandboxes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Most providers leave "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"capabilities.network"},{"type":"text","value":" to the harness. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" also enforces it on the host HTTP/HTTPS proxy ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"networkPolicy: true"},{"type":"text","value":")."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx policy"},{"type":"text","value":" can allow or deny hosts. It has no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":". Deny wins over allow, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"network: 'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":" is an allowlist, not a full deny."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"capabilities.network"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" writes"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No policy and no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the policy list is empty, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" runs "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx policy init deny-all"},{"type":"text","value":". A known adapter ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grok-build"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"claude-code"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"codex"},{"type":"text","value":") then writes its model API host and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" as a per-sandbox allow on top of that deny-all. Unknown adapters stay on the machine preset."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No policy and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowNetwork"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-sandbox allow of the model API host (when the adapter is known), "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":", then apply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No policy and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":" only"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Per-sandbox deny of those hosts. Allow stays empty. No auto hosts and no "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":". This is additive deny on the machine preset."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Allow "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"**"},{"type":"text","value":", then apply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Allow the model API host, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":", then apply "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" (or the policy "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default"},{"type":"text","value":" when "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"network"},{"type":"text","value":" is unset)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Same allowlist as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":". The harness still asks for tools and commands."}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Auto-allowed model hosts:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grok-build"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"api.x.ai"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"claude-code"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"api.anthropic.com"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"codex"},{"type":"text","value":" → "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"api.openai.com"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"opencode"},{"type":"text","value":" or an unknown adapter → none"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If the allowlist would be empty under "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":", create throws. Pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allowNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":", or use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grokBuildText"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"claudeCodeText"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"codexText"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The guest still dials "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"host.docker.internal"},{"type":"text","value":" for the tool bridge. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":" proxy rewrites that host to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" before the policy match. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbxSandbox()"},{"type":"text","value":" adds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" when it writes a real allowlist. It does not add "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"localhost"},{"type":"text","value":" for "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"denyNetwork"},{"type":"text","value":" only."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: sbxSandbox({\n    allowNetwork: ['*.npmjs.org', 'registry.npmjs.org'],\n  }),\n  policy: defineSandboxPolicy({\n    capabilities: { network: 'deny' },\n    commands: { allow: ['pnpm test'], deny: ['sudo *'] },\n  }),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Command rules and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fileWrite"},{"type":"text","value":" stay with the harness. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sbx"},{"type":"text","value":" does not see command lines."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"precedence-deny-ask-allow","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Precedence: deny > ask > allow"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When more than one rule could match an action, the strictest wins. The order is\n"},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":" > "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" > "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"}]},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If any matching rule says "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":", the action is blocked and no other rule\noverrides it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Otherwise, if any matching rule says "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":", the action requires approval."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Otherwise, if a rule says "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":", it runs."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If nothing matches, the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default"},{"type":"text","value":" decision applies."}]}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    // `curl` is allowed broadly…\n    allow: ['curl *'],\n    // …but `deny` wins, so this specific host is always blocked.\n    deny: ['curl * internal.example.com*'],\n  },\n  default: 'deny',\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This means you can paint with a broad "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":" and carve exceptions out with\nnarrower "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":" patterns, confident the exceptions take priority."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"the-default","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The default"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default"},{"type":"text","value":" is the decision for anything none of your rules match. Set it to the\nposture you want at the edges:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default: 'allow'"},{"type":"text","value":" is permissive: only the things you explicitly "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" about or\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":" are gated. Reasonable for trusted dev loops."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default: 'ask'"},{"type":"text","value":" is cautious: unknown actions pause for approval. A good middle\nground."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default: 'deny'"},{"type":"text","value":", locked down: the agent can only run what you explicitly\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":". Strongest posture for untrusted or production runs."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When omitted, treat the default as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" so unforeseen actions surface rather\nthan silently running."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-ask-surfaces","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" surfaces"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" decision is not a guess the SDK makes, it's a question routed back to\nyou. When the agent attempts an "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":"-gated action, the harness pauses it and\nemits an "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"approval request"}]},{"type":"text","value":" into the run stream. Your client answers that\nrequest (approve or reject), and the harness either lets the action proceed or\nblocks it based on the answer. Until the client responds, the action is held."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This is why "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" is the right choice for actions that are usually fine but\noccasionally dangerous ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm install"},{"type":"text","value":" pulling a new dependency, an outbound\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"curl"},{"type":"text","value":"): the human stays in the loop without you having to enumerate every safe\ncommand up front."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"how-adapters-map-a-policy","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How adapters map a policy"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A policy is portable. Each harness adapter translates the same\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"allow"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ask"},{"type":"text","value":" / "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"deny"},{"type":"text","value":" description into its own native permission system:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Grok Build harness maps it onto the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grok"},{"type":"text","value":" CLI's permission flags."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Claude Code harness maps it onto Claude Code's permission rules\n(allowed/ask/denied tool and command rules)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Codex harness maps it onto Codex's approval and sandbox settings."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Other harnesses map it onto whatever native gate they expose."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Where a harness can't express a particular rule, it degrades rather than\nfailing the run, the unsupported rule is skipped (with a warning) instead of\nthrowing. Because the mapping is the adapter's job, you write the policy once\nand it behaves consistently no matter which provider or harness runs the\nsandbox."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A deny-only list with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"default: 'allow'"},{"type":"text","value":" stays permissive on Grok Build and\nCodex. Those harnesses keep auto-approve. They do not enforce\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"commands.deny"},{"type":"text","value":". Isolation is the outer sandbox. Use Claude Code when you\nneed command-level deny."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some providers run as a non-root user, so package installs in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"setup"},{"type":"text","value":" need\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo"},{"type":"text","value":". Do not deny "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"sudo *"},{"type":"text","value":" on those providers. See "},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nfor the list, and "},{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for how to write setup commands."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"wiring-it-on","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Wiring it on"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A policy does nothing on its own, it takes effect when you attach it to a\nsandbox. Pass it as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"policy"},{"type":"text","value":" on "},{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineSandbox"}]},{"type":"text","value":"; from there it\nguards every run that uses that sandbox, including the "},{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":"\nsetup commands and any host "},{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":" bridged into the agent."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { defineSandboxPolicy, defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n  capabilities: { fileWrite: 'allow', network: 'ask' },\n  default: 'ask',\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  policy,\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"next-steps","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Next steps"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./providers","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Providers"}]},{"type":"text","value":", attach the policy via "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"defineSandbox"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./workspace","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workspace"}]},{"type":"text","value":", the setup commands and scripts the policy guards."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./tools","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tools"}]},{"type":"text","value":", host tools bridged into the agent run under the same policy."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"./lifecycle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Lifecycle"}]},{"type":"text","value":", how a guarded sandbox resumes and tears down."}]}]}]}],"headings":[{"id":"decisions","text":"Decisions","level":2},{"id":"commands","text":"Commands","level":2},{"id":"glob-patterns","text":"Glob patterns","level":3},{"id":"capabilities","text":"Capabilities","level":2},{"id":"network-policy-on-docker-sandboxes","text":"Network policy on Docker Sandboxes","level":2},{"id":"precedence-deny-ask-allow","text":"Precedence: deny > ask > allow","level":2},{"id":"the-default","text":"The default","level":2},{"id":"how-ask-surfaces","text":"How ask surfaces","level":2},{"id":"how-adapters-map-a-policy","text":"How adapters map a policy","level":2},{"id":"wiring-it-on","text":"Wiring it on","level":2},{"id":"next-steps","text":"Next steps","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA policy is your guardrail layer: it decides which commands and capabilities the\nin-sandbox agent may run outright, must ask about first, or can never run.\n`defineSandboxPolicy()` describes those rules once, portably, and each\n[provider](./providers)'s harness adapter maps them onto its own native\npermission system. You attach a policy to a sandbox via\n[`defineSandbox({ policy })`](./providers), where it guards the commands the\n[workspace](./workspace) setup and bridged [tools](./tools) run.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy, defineSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n  capabilities: { fileWrite: 'allow', network: 'ask' },\n  default: 'ask',\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  policy,\n})\n```\n\n## Decisions\n\nEvery command or capability resolves to one of three decisions:\n\n| Decision | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `allow` | The agent runs it without interruption. |\n| `ask`   | The agent pauses; the harness emits an approval request the client answers before the action proceeds. |\n| `deny`  | The action is blocked outright. The agent cannot run it. |\n\n## Commands\n\n`commands` holds three lists of command patterns: `allow`, `ask` and `deny`.\nA pattern matches against the command the agent is about to run:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Glob patterns\n\nPatterns support `*` globs, so you can gate a whole family of commands with one\nentry. `curl *` matches any `curl` invocation; `sudo *` matches anything run\nthrough `sudo`. An exact string like `pnpm test` matches only that command.\nPrefer the named [workspace scripts](./workspace) (`pnpm test`, `pnpm build`)\nin your `allow` list. They give the policy stable names to match rather than\nfreeform shell.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n`capabilities` applies the same `allow` / `ask` / `deny` decisions to\ncoarse-grained abilities rather than individual commands, for example\nfilesystem writes or outbound network access:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  capabilities: {\n    fileWrite: 'allow', // let the agent edit the working tree freely\n    network: 'ask',     // pause for approval before any outbound request\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThis is the broad backstop: even if a specific network command isn't in your\n`commands` lists, `network: 'ask'` still forces an approval for anything that\nreaches out.\n\nSome providers also enforce network at create time when `network: 'deny'`. See\n[Providers](./providers) for that mapping.\n\n## Network policy on Docker Sandboxes\n\nMost providers leave `capabilities.network` to the harness. `sbxSandbox()` also enforces it on the host HTTP/HTTPS proxy (`networkPolicy: true`).\n\n`sbx policy` can allow or deny hosts. It has no `ask`. Deny wins over allow, so `network: 'deny'` is an allowlist, not a full deny.\n\n| TanStack `capabilities.network` | What `sbxSandbox()` writes |\n| --- | --- |\n| No policy and no `allowNetwork` / `denyNetwork` | If the policy list is empty, `sbxSandbox()` runs `sbx policy init deny-all`. A known adapter (`grok-build`, `claude-code`, `codex`) then writes its model API host and `localhost` as a per-sandbox allow on top of that deny-all. Unknown adapters stay on the machine preset. |\n| No policy and `allowNetwork` | Per-sandbox allow of the model API host (when the adapter is known), `localhost`, and `allowNetwork`, then apply `denyNetwork`. |\n| No policy and `denyNetwork` only | Per-sandbox deny of those hosts. Allow stays empty. No auto hosts and no `localhost`. This is additive deny on the machine preset. |\n| `allow` | Allow `**`, then apply `denyNetwork`. |\n| `deny` | Allow the model API host, `localhost`, and `allowNetwork`, then apply `denyNetwork`. |\n| `ask` (or the policy `default` when `network` is unset) | Same allowlist as `deny`. The harness still asks for tools and commands. |\n\nAuto-allowed model hosts:\n\n- `grok-build` → `api.x.ai`\n- `claude-code` → `api.anthropic.com`\n- `codex` → `api.openai.com`\n- `opencode` or an unknown adapter → none\n\nIf the allowlist would be empty under `deny` or `ask`, create throws. Pass `allowNetwork`, or use `grokBuildText` / `claudeCodeText` / `codexText`.\n\nThe guest still dials `host.docker.internal` for the tool bridge. The `sbx` proxy rewrites that host to `localhost` before the policy match. `sbxSandbox()` adds `localhost` when it writes a real allowlist. It does not add `localhost` for `denyNetwork` only.\n\n```ts\nimport { sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: sbxSandbox({\n    allowNetwork: ['*.npmjs.org', 'registry.npmjs.org'],\n  }),\n  policy: defineSandboxPolicy({\n    capabilities: { network: 'deny' },\n    commands: { allow: ['pnpm test'], deny: ['sudo *'] },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\nCommand rules and `fileWrite` stay with the harness. `sbx` does not see command lines.\n\n## Precedence: deny > ask > allow\n\nWhen more than one rule could match an action, the strictest wins. The order is\n**`deny` > `ask` > `allow`**:\n\n- If any matching rule says `deny`, the action is blocked and no other rule\n  overrides it.\n- Otherwise, if any matching rule says `ask`, the action requires approval.\n- Otherwise, if a rule says `allow`, it runs.\n- If nothing matches, the `default` decision applies.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    // `curl` is allowed broadly…\n    allow: ['curl *'],\n    // …but `deny` wins, so this specific host is always blocked.\n    deny: ['curl * internal.example.com*'],\n  },\n  default: 'deny',\n})\n```\n\nThis means you can paint with a broad `allow` and carve exceptions out with\nnarrower `ask` / `deny` patterns, confident the exceptions take priority.\n\n## The default\n\n`default` is the decision for anything none of your rules match. Set it to the\nposture you want at the edges:\n\n- `default: 'allow'` is permissive: only the things you explicitly `ask` about or\n  `deny` are gated. Reasonable for trusted dev loops.\n- `default: 'ask'` is cautious: unknown actions pause for approval. A good middle\n  ground.\n- `default: 'deny'`, locked down: the agent can only run what you explicitly\n  `allow`. Strongest posture for untrusted or production runs.\n\nWhen omitted, treat the default as `ask` so unforeseen actions surface rather\nthan silently running.\n\n## How `ask` surfaces\n\nAn `ask` decision is not a guess the SDK makes, it's a question routed back to\nyou. When the agent attempts an `ask`-gated action, the harness pauses it and\nemits an **approval request** into the run stream. Your client answers that\nrequest (approve or reject), and the harness either lets the action proceed or\nblocks it based on the answer. Until the client responds, the action is held.\n\nThis is why `ask` is the right choice for actions that are usually fine but\noccasionally dangerous (`pnpm install` pulling a new dependency, an outbound\n`curl`): the human stays in the loop without you having to enumerate every safe\ncommand up front.\n\n## How adapters map a policy\n\nA policy is portable. Each harness adapter translates the same\n`allow` / `ask` / `deny` description into its own native permission system:\n\n- A Grok Build harness maps it onto the `grok` CLI's permission flags.\n- A Claude Code harness maps it onto Claude Code's permission rules\n  (allowed/ask/denied tool and command rules).\n- A Codex harness maps it onto Codex's approval and sandbox settings.\n- Other harnesses map it onto whatever native gate they expose.\n\nWhere a harness can't express a particular rule, it degrades rather than\nfailing the run, the unsupported rule is skipped (with a warning) instead of\nthrowing. Because the mapping is the adapter's job, you write the policy once\nand it behaves consistently no matter which provider or harness runs the\nsandbox.\n\nA deny-only list with `default: 'allow'` stays permissive on Grok Build and\nCodex. Those harnesses keep auto-approve. They do not enforce\n`commands.deny`. Isolation is the outer sandbox. Use Claude Code when you\nneed command-level deny.\n\nSome providers run as a non-root user, so package installs in `setup` need\n`sudo`. Do not deny `sudo *` on those providers. See [Providers](./providers)\nfor the list, and [Workspace](./workspace) for how to write setup commands.\n\n## Wiring it on\n\nA policy does nothing on its own, it takes effect when you attach it to a\nsandbox. Pass it as `policy` on [`defineSandbox`](./providers); from there it\nguards every run that uses that sandbox, including the [workspace](./workspace)\nsetup commands and any host [tools](./tools) bridged into the agent.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy, defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n  capabilities: { fileWrite: 'allow', network: 'ask' },\n  default: 'ask',\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  policy,\n})\n```\n\n## Next steps\n\n- [Providers](./providers), attach the policy via `defineSandbox`.\n- [Workspace](./workspace), the setup commands and scripts the policy guards.\n- [Tools](./tools), host tools bridged into the agent run under the same policy.\n- [Lifecycle](./lifecycle), how a guarded sandbox resumes and tears down.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Policy\nid: policy\norder: 7\ndescription: \"Set allow/ask/deny guardrails over the commands and capabilities the in-sandbox agent may run, ask about, or never run, in one portable description that each harness maps onto its native permissions.\"\n---\n\nA policy is your guardrail layer: it decides which commands and capabilities the\nin-sandbox agent may run outright, must ask about first, or can never run.\n`defineSandboxPolicy()` describes those rules once, portably, and each\n[provider](./providers)'s harness adapter maps them onto its own native\npermission system. You attach a policy to a sandbox via\n[`defineSandbox({ policy })`](./providers), where it guards the commands the\n[workspace](./workspace) setup and bridged [tools](./tools) run.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy, defineSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n  capabilities: { fileWrite: 'allow', network: 'ask' },\n  default: 'ask',\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  policy,\n})\n```\n\n## Decisions\n\nEvery command or capability resolves to one of three decisions:\n\n| Decision | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| `allow` | The agent runs it without interruption. |\n| `ask`   | The agent pauses; the harness emits an approval request the client answers before the action proceeds. |\n| `deny`  | The action is blocked outright. The agent cannot run it. |\n\n## Commands\n\n`commands` holds three lists of command patterns: `allow`, `ask` and `deny`.\nA pattern matches against the command the agent is about to run:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n})\n```\n\n### Glob patterns\n\nPatterns support `*` globs, so you can gate a whole family of commands with one\nentry. `curl *` matches any `curl` invocation; `sudo *` matches anything run\nthrough `sudo`. An exact string like `pnpm test` matches only that command.\nPrefer the named [workspace scripts](./workspace) (`pnpm test`, `pnpm build`)\nin your `allow` list. They give the policy stable names to match rather than\nfreeform shell.\n\n## Capabilities\n\n`capabilities` applies the same `allow` / `ask` / `deny` decisions to\ncoarse-grained abilities rather than individual commands, for example\nfilesystem writes or outbound network access:\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  capabilities: {\n    fileWrite: 'allow', // let the agent edit the working tree freely\n    network: 'ask',     // pause for approval before any outbound request\n  },\n})\n```\n\nThis is the broad backstop: even if a specific network command isn't in your\n`commands` lists, `network: 'ask'` still forces an approval for anything that\nreaches out.\n\nSome providers also enforce network at create time when `network: 'deny'`. See\n[Providers](./providers) for that mapping.\n\n## Network policy on Docker Sandboxes\n\nMost providers leave `capabilities.network` to the harness. `sbxSandbox()` also enforces it on the host HTTP/HTTPS proxy (`networkPolicy: true`).\n\n`sbx policy` can allow or deny hosts. It has no `ask`. Deny wins over allow, so `network: 'deny'` is an allowlist, not a full deny.\n\n| TanStack `capabilities.network` | What `sbxSandbox()` writes |\n| --- | --- |\n| No policy and no `allowNetwork` / `denyNetwork` | If the policy list is empty, `sbxSandbox()` runs `sbx policy init deny-all`. A known adapter (`grok-build`, `claude-code`, `codex`) then writes its model API host and `localhost` as a per-sandbox allow on top of that deny-all. Unknown adapters stay on the machine preset. |\n| No policy and `allowNetwork` | Per-sandbox allow of the model API host (when the adapter is known), `localhost`, and `allowNetwork`, then apply `denyNetwork`. |\n| No policy and `denyNetwork` only | Per-sandbox deny of those hosts. Allow stays empty. No auto hosts and no `localhost`. This is additive deny on the machine preset. |\n| `allow` | Allow `**`, then apply `denyNetwork`. |\n| `deny` | Allow the model API host, `localhost`, and `allowNetwork`, then apply `denyNetwork`. |\n| `ask` (or the policy `default` when `network` is unset) | Same allowlist as `deny`. The harness still asks for tools and commands. |\n\nAuto-allowed model hosts:\n\n- `grok-build` → `api.x.ai`\n- `claude-code` → `api.anthropic.com`\n- `codex` → `api.openai.com`\n- `opencode` or an unknown adapter → none\n\nIf the allowlist would be empty under `deny` or `ask`, create throws. Pass `allowNetwork`, or use `grokBuildText` / `claudeCodeText` / `codexText`.\n\nThe guest still dials `host.docker.internal` for the tool bridge. The `sbx` proxy rewrites that host to `localhost` before the policy match. `sbxSandbox()` adds `localhost` when it writes a real allowlist. It does not add `localhost` for `denyNetwork` only.\n\n```ts\nimport { sbxSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\nimport { defineSandbox, defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: sbxSandbox({\n    allowNetwork: ['*.npmjs.org', 'registry.npmjs.org'],\n  }),\n  policy: defineSandboxPolicy({\n    capabilities: { network: 'deny' },\n    commands: { allow: ['pnpm test'], deny: ['sudo *'] },\n  }),\n})\n```\n\nCommand rules and `fileWrite` stay with the harness. `sbx` does not see command lines.\n\n## Precedence: deny > ask > allow\n\nWhen more than one rule could match an action, the strictest wins. The order is\n**`deny` > `ask` > `allow`**:\n\n- If any matching rule says `deny`, the action is blocked and no other rule\n  overrides it.\n- Otherwise, if any matching rule says `ask`, the action requires approval.\n- Otherwise, if a rule says `allow`, it runs.\n- If nothing matches, the `default` decision applies.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    // `curl` is allowed broadly…\n    allow: ['curl *'],\n    // …but `deny` wins, so this specific host is always blocked.\n    deny: ['curl * internal.example.com*'],\n  },\n  default: 'deny',\n})\n```\n\nThis means you can paint with a broad `allow` and carve exceptions out with\nnarrower `ask` / `deny` patterns, confident the exceptions take priority.\n\n## The default\n\n`default` is the decision for anything none of your rules match. Set it to the\nposture you want at the edges:\n\n- `default: 'allow'` is permissive: only the things you explicitly `ask` about or\n  `deny` are gated. Reasonable for trusted dev loops.\n- `default: 'ask'` is cautious: unknown actions pause for approval. A good middle\n  ground.\n- `default: 'deny'`, locked down: the agent can only run what you explicitly\n  `allow`. Strongest posture for untrusted or production runs.\n\nWhen omitted, treat the default as `ask` so unforeseen actions surface rather\nthan silently running.\n\n## How `ask` surfaces\n\nAn `ask` decision is not a guess the SDK makes, it's a question routed back to\nyou. When the agent attempts an `ask`-gated action, the harness pauses it and\nemits an **approval request** into the run stream. Your client answers that\nrequest (approve or reject), and the harness either lets the action proceed or\nblocks it based on the answer. Until the client responds, the action is held.\n\nThis is why `ask` is the right choice for actions that are usually fine but\noccasionally dangerous (`pnpm install` pulling a new dependency, an outbound\n`curl`): the human stays in the loop without you having to enumerate every safe\ncommand up front.\n\n## How adapters map a policy\n\nA policy is portable. Each harness adapter translates the same\n`allow` / `ask` / `deny` description into its own native permission system:\n\n- A Grok Build harness maps it onto the `grok` CLI's permission flags.\n- A Claude Code harness maps it onto Claude Code's permission rules\n  (allowed/ask/denied tool and command rules).\n- A Codex harness maps it onto Codex's approval and sandbox settings.\n- Other harnesses map it onto whatever native gate they expose.\n\nWhere a harness can't express a particular rule, it degrades rather than\nfailing the run, the unsupported rule is skipped (with a warning) instead of\nthrowing. Because the mapping is the adapter's job, you write the policy once\nand it behaves consistently no matter which provider or harness runs the\nsandbox.\n\nA deny-only list with `default: 'allow'` stays permissive on Grok Build and\nCodex. Those harnesses keep auto-approve. They do not enforce\n`commands.deny`. Isolation is the outer sandbox. Use Claude Code when you\nneed command-level deny.\n\nSome providers run as a non-root user, so package installs in `setup` need\n`sudo`. Do not deny `sudo *` on those providers. See [Providers](./providers)\nfor the list, and [Workspace](./workspace) for how to write setup commands.\n\n## Wiring it on\n\nA policy does nothing on its own, it takes effect when you attach it to a\nsandbox. Pass it as `policy` on [`defineSandbox`](./providers); from there it\nguards every run that uses that sandbox, including the [workspace](./workspace)\nsetup commands and any host [tools](./tools) bridged into the agent.\n\n```ts\nimport { defineSandboxPolicy, defineSandbox, defineWorkspace, githubRepo } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox'\nimport { dockerSandbox } from '@tanstack/ai-sandbox-docker'\n\nconst policy = defineSandboxPolicy({\n  commands: {\n    allow: ['pnpm test', 'pnpm typecheck', 'git diff'],\n    ask: ['pnpm install', 'curl *'],\n    deny: ['sudo *', 'rm -rf *'],\n  },\n  capabilities: { fileWrite: 'allow', network: 'ask' },\n  default: 'ask',\n})\n\nconst sandbox = defineSandbox({\n  id: 'repo-agent',\n  provider: dockerSandbox({ image: 'node:22' }),\n  workspace: defineWorkspace({\n    source: githubRepo({ repo: 'owner/app' }),\n    setup: ['corepack enable', 'pnpm install'],\n  }),\n  policy,\n})\n```\n\n## Next steps\n\n- [Providers](./providers), attach the policy via `defineSandbox`.\n- [Workspace](./workspace), the setup commands and scripts the policy guards.\n- [Tools](./tools), host tools bridged into the agent run under the same policy.\n- [Lifecycle](./lifecycle), how a guarded sandbox resumes and tears down.\n"}
